<!--
  The launcher mark is the wordmark in its rule — the brand's actual logo,
  not a monogram derived from it.

  An earlier version used a single Courier "P". It was legible, but Courier is a
  typewriter face, so a lone serifed letter reads as a document or a writing app
  rather than apparel. Premium clothing apps overwhelmingly put the whole
  wordmark on a plain tile (The Row, Aesop, SSENSE, Jil Sander); the letters
  being small is accepted, because the tile itself is the recognition cue.

  Every glyph is extracted from the shipped CourierPrime-Bold.ttf and placed by
  transform, mirrored Es included — the same construction as Wordmark.kt, so
  the icon and the in-app mark cannot drift apart.

  Geometry: the rule is 64x18dp centred in the 108dp viewport, which keeps its
  corners inside the circular mask (furthest corner sits 33.2dp from centre
  against a 36dp radius). Cap height lands at 4.3dp — small by design, and the
  reason the rule is there: it frames the type so a short line reads as a mark
  rather than as stray text.
-->
